welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Elizabeth wood a professor of  political science at Yale University  professor woods current research focuses  on sexual violence during war she is the  author of insurgent collective action  and civil war in El Salvador and forging  democracy from below insurgent  transitions in South Africa and El  Salvador as well as various scholarly  articles today we'll talk with Professor  wood about two of her recently published  works on sexual violence during war  welcome professor wood thanks very much  for having me you have researched sexual  violence occurring in wars throughout  the world what is your most important  finding yes I visited Sri Lanka Israel  Palestine South Africa Peru Colombia all  for this project on wartime sexual  violence the most important finding for  me was also the most surprising finding  in my work on this subject so far and  that is that there are armed groups that  don't engage in widespread sexual  violence indeed there are some conflicts  where neither party to the conflict  engages in sexual violence so there's  really an absence of sexual violence in  some conflicts why do you think that is  can you cite some examples sure there  various reasons why an armed group might  not engage in sexual violence so for  example the leadership of a group might  decide it is against their strategic  interests or against their normative  beliefs if they're combatants engage in  sexual violence there might be a number  of reasons for example they might depend  on civilians for high-quality  intelligence and might reason that if  they engage in widespread sexual  violence against those civilians  civilians will stop providing such  intelligence but whether or not that  message gets down to the combatants on  the ground of course depends on the  quality of the  pterri hierarchy so that's another  condition for an absence of sexual  violence but there may also be on the  ground dynamics going on in other cases  so for example if combatants believe for  example for religious really reasons  that engaging in sexual violence across  some ethnic cleavage would be polluting  to themselves then they may not engage  in sexual violence for that reason I say  why is it important to study sexual  violence during wartime sexual violence  is a form of violence against some of  the most vulnerable populations in war  zones  the most typical case of sexual violence  is rape of girls and women's sometimes  repeated over an extended period of time  and those populations are often  defenseless often extremely poor often  with very few resources to try to  overcome this horrible event and sexual  violence may have very long run  consequences not just for the victims of  sexual violence but for the social  fabric of communities but if I'm right  that sexual violence varies this the way  that I described that there are armed  groups that do not engage in sexual  violence it's also very important that  we document and analyze that because  that fact if I'm right has important  policy implications why because if there  are armed groups that don't engage in  sexual violence that means that  leadership's  if they really want to can prohibit  sexual violence and therefore we have a  position we have evidence against this  idea that sexual violence is endemic  during war it's just part of the horrors  of war and therefore we have more  grounds to hold responsible those groups  that do engage in sexual violence okay  so in doing your research you have found  variations in the types of sexual  violence from war to war can you cite  some specific examples sure  so the type of sexual violence in some  settings is the rape for example in an  afternoon of the girls and women in some  village for example but it may also take  the form of sexual slavery where those  girls and women may be taken essentially  as slave by an armed group and held  forcibly sometimes for not just months  but years sometimes it takes the form of  sexual torture that is torture that has  various sexual dimensions and that is  actually quite common against men and  boys as well as against girls and women  the I should note also that who carries  out sexual violence varies across these  conflicts in some conflicts it's really  agents of the state that engage in  widespread sexual violence but in other  settings it's non-state groups that do  so and let me mention two more  particular patterns that are maybe not  widely understood one is that the  prevalence of gang rape in war is much  much higher than gang rape during  peacetime and also shockingly I think to  many people women engage in sexual  violence as well in some settings it's  not just men Wow can you I'm sitting  here wondering if there are any  particular countries that you have found  to be particularly brutal in terms of  evidence of sexual violence  I think the countries that are most  brutal in terms of the patterns of  sexual violence are countries that I  have not visited so among contemporary  conflicts I think I would rank probably  above all the rest the the Democratic  Republic of the Congo  in the eastern part of that country  there are ongoing patterns of conflict  that include a very high level of sexual  violence  and that is on a scale that we see very  rarely also in Darfur there's been a  very high level of sexual violence so  those are two conflicts that I'm relying  on my colleagues in the community of  nongovernmental organizations and  international organizations who document  that violence I'm relying on their data  and documents to understand what's going  on there let's talk a little bit about  your methodology because I imagine that  it's very difficult to gather data to  analyze this particular type of violence  tell me about your methodology you're  absolutely right it's one of the most  difficult things to document in the  social sciences and I use essentially  two methods the first method is that I  rely on nongovernmental organizations  government agencies international  organizations to document the patterns  of sexual violence I don't try to do  that myself and the kinds of data that  have been gathered essentially are of  two types one is eyewitness reports  sometimes from victims sometimes from  people who witnessed sexual violence and  also in some settings we have medical  reports or medical surveys the of course  the there are many risks here to victims  of sexual violence additional risks in  many settings of sexual violence report  what happened to them they may be either  stigmatized by their own community or  they may be targeted by armed groups  still in the area  indeed even witnesses to sexual violence  if they talk run some risk and moreover  in many cultural settings victims of  sexual violence be they women who've  suffered rape or gang rape or girls  particularly or be they boys or men who  have suffered sexual torture it is  extremely difficult in many settings for  them to talk about their experience  and indeed it seems that some victims of  sexual violence may become reach Rama  ties through the retelling of what  happened to them the conditions for  doing ethical research through  interviews with witnesses and victims of  sexual violence are I think very narrow  so I don't in fact myself  interview victims of sexual violence I  rely on the data and documents gathered  by others in addition to gathering the  data and documents from these other  organizations I also interview their  staff members extensively in these  various countries the other method that  I use is to study the internal dynamics  of the armed group so I gather again all  the data and documents analyses that I  can of the armed groups that I'm looking  at but ideally at the heart of what I'm  doing is interviewing former combatants  about how they were recruited how are  they trained how was discipline within  the group carried out in particular if  someone in their small unit engaged in  violence against civilians was that  promoted was it punished was it ignored  and so on thank you very much for  sharing some of your research with us  today thanks Marilyn it's been a  pleasure for more information about  Professor wood and her work please visit  our website at Yale edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you
 

